          "comments": [
            {
              "originalPosition": 127,
              "body": "wanted to use it as a guideline as we received feedback that BusBW algo_factor was  ambiguous ,  and makes sense since the whole point of BusBW is being algorithm-invariant. The original definition contradicted this and may  produce inconsistent reports.\r\n\r\nSee these examples from the previous definition-> \r\n\r\n-> The same hardware moving the same bytes in the same  time can report different BusBW just because NCCL switched from tree to ring at the size-threshold boundary.\r\n-> A library upgrade (e.g., NCCL 2.18 \u2192 2.22) re-tunes thresholds and shifts the BusBW curve without any hardware change.\r\n-> A vendor that ships a custom CCL with a more aggressive tree algorithm appears \"faster\" in BusBW terms even when on-wire bytes per second are identical.\r\n\r\nSo on this new definition a   fixed-ring algo_factor convention considers these situations, , so  for example benchmarkiing two fabrics / DUTs  that move the same data in the same time provide same results.",
              "createdAt": "2026-05-06T13:18:55Z",
              "updatedAt": "2026-05-06T13:18:55Z"
            }
          ]
        },
